@namespace RizzyUI.Docs.Components.Pages.Interactivity

@using RizzyUI
@using RizzyUI.Docs.Components.Pages.Interactivity.Examples

<PageTitle>Your First Alpine Code-Behind</PageTitle>

<RzQuickReferenceContainer>
    <RzArticle ProseWidth="ProseWidth.UltraWide">
        <SideContent>
            <RzQuickReference />
        </SideContent>
        <MainContent>
            <RzBreadcrumb class="mb-4 not-prose">
                <BreadcrumbList>
                    <BreadcrumbItem>
                        <BreadcrumbLink Href="/" hx-boost="true" hx-select="#content" hx-target="#content" hx-swap="outerHTML">Docs</BreadcrumbLink>
                    </BreadcrumbItem>
                    <BreadcrumbSeparator />
                    <BreadcrumbItem>
                        Interactivity
                    </BreadcrumbItem>
                    <BreadcrumbSeparator />
                    <BreadcrumbItem>
                        <BreadcrumbPage>Your First Code-Behind</BreadcrumbPage>
                    </BreadcrumbItem>
                </BreadcrumbList>
            </RzBreadcrumb>
            <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H1" QuickReferenceTitle="Your First Alpine Code-Behind">
                Your First Alpine Code-Behind with <em>RzAlpineComponent</em>
            </RzHeading>

            <RzParagraph class="lead">
                A code-behind module keeps a component's client-side behavior in a JavaScript file next to the
                component: <code>Counter.razor</code> for the markup, <code>Counter.razor.js</code> for the state and
                methods. The module loads only on pages that use the component, works after htmx swaps, and runs
                under a strict Content Security Policy.
            </RzParagraph>

            <RzSeparator />

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="The two files">
                    The two files
                </RzHeading>
                <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6 my-6 text-foreground">
                    <li><strong><code>Counter.razor</code></strong>: the markup, with <code>@@attribute [RzAlpineCodeBehind]</code> at the top and the markup wrapped in <code>&lt;RzAlpineComponent For="this"&gt;</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>Counter.razor.js</code></strong>, in the same folder: a module whose default export returns the component's data (state and methods).</li>
                </ol>
                <RzParagraph>
                    To create both, run <code>dotnet new rizzyui-codebehind -n Counter</code> in the folder (from the
                    <code>RizzyUI.Templates</code> package; add <code>--typescript</code> for a <code>.razor.ts</code> module).
                </RzParagraph>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="Step 1: The component">
                    Step 1: The component
                </RzHeading>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.Razor" ViewerTitle="Counter.razor" class="mt-4">
@@attribute [RzAlpineCodeBehind]

&lt;RzAlpineComponent For=&quot;this&quot; AsChild&gt;
  &lt;div class=&quot;flex items-center gap-4&quot;&gt;
    &lt;RzButton x-on:click=&quot;decrement&quot;&gt;-&lt;/RzButton&gt;
    &lt;span x-text=&quot;count&quot; class=&quot;font-mono text-lg&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;RzButton x-on:click=&quot;increment&quot;&gt;+&lt;/RzButton&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/RzAlpineComponent&gt;
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 my-6 text-foreground">
                    <li><code>[RzAlpineCodeBehind]</code> records where the component's source file is, so RizzyUI can find <code>Counter.razor.js</code> next to it. In a <code>Counter.razor.cs</code> file, put <code>[RzAlpineCodeBehind]</code> on the class instead. The build warns (<code>RZUI1001</code>) when the module file is missing.</li>
                    <li><code>For="this"</code> tells <code>RzAlpineComponent</code> which component's module to load.</li>
                    <li><code>AsChild</code> puts the Alpine attributes on the child element instead of adding a wrapper <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>. The child's own attributes (such as its <code>id</code>) are kept.</li>
                    <li><code>Name</code> is optional. RizzyUI generates the <code>x-data</code> name from the component type and the module's URL (for example <code>rzcb_Counter_1a2b3c4d</code>), so two components can never collide. Set it only when other markup refers to the component by name.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="Step 2: The module">
                    Step 2: The module
                </RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    The default export is a function that returns a new data object for each instance of the component.
                    Properties are reactive state; methods are what the markup calls.
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.JavaScript" ViewerTitle="Counter.razor.js" class="mt-4">
export default () =&gt; ({
  count: 0,

  increment() {
    this.count++;
  },

  decrement() {
    this.count--;
  },
});
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzParagraph>
                    <strong>What the page gets:</strong> the root element carries <code>x-data</code> (the name),
                    <code>x-load</code> (when to load, <code>eager</code> by default) and <code>x-load-src</code> (the
                    module's URL). Alpine loads the module when the strategy allows, registers its default export under
                    the name, and starts the component. No inline script is rendered, so this works the same in the
                    initial page, in content swapped in by htmx, and under a Content Security Policy without
                    <code>'unsafe-inline'</code>.
                </RzParagraph>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="Step 3: Passing data from C#">
                    Step 3: Passing data from C#
                </RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    The <code>Props</code> parameter takes any JSON-serializable object, including anonymous types.
                    It is serialized with web defaults, so property names arrive in camelCase:
                    <code>InitialCount</code> becomes <code>initialCount</code>.
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzBrowser Layout="typeof(PreviewLayout)">
                    <div class="mx-auto flex items-center justify-center gap-4 p-8 min-h-20">
                        <CounterWithProps />
                    </div>
                </RzBrowser>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.Razor" ViewerTitle="CounterWithProps.razor" class="mt-4">
@@attribute [RzAlpineCodeBehind]

&lt;RzAlpineComponent For=&quot;this&quot; AsChild Props=&quot;@@(new { InitialCount = 10, Step = 2 })&quot;&gt;
  &lt;div class=&quot;flex items-center gap-4&quot;&gt;
    &lt;RzButton x-on:click=&quot;decrement&quot;&gt;-&lt;/RzButton&gt;
    &lt;span x-text=&quot;count&quot; class=&quot;font-mono text-lg&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;RzButton x-on:click=&quot;increment&quot;&gt;+&lt;/RzButton&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/RzAlpineComponent&gt;
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzParagraph class="mt-4">
                    In the module, read them with <code>this.$props</code>, in <code>init()</code> or any other method.
                    In markup, <code>$props</code> works too (<code>x-text="$props.title"</code>). The props object is
                    shared and read-only: copy values into the component's own state to change them.
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.JavaScript" ViewerTitle="CounterWithProps.razor.js" class="mt-4">
export default () =&gt; ({
  count: 0,
  step: 1,

  init() {
    this.count = this.$props.initialCount;
    this.step = this.$props.step;
  },

  increment() {
    this.count += this.step;
  },

  decrement() {
    this.count -= this.step;
  },
});
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzParagraph class="mt-4">
                    <code>Rizzy.props(this.$el)</code> returns the same object, for code outside Alpine methods.
                </RzParagraph>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="Editor support and type checking">
                    Editor support and type checking
                </RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    RizzyUI ships TypeScript declarations for modules. The NuGet package copies them to
                    <code>obj/rizzyui/rizzyui.d.ts</code> on build; a <code>jsconfig.json</code> that maps the name
                    <code>rizzyui</code> to that file (the starter template has one) gives your editor completion for
                    <code>this.$props</code>, Alpine's magics and the <code>Rizzy</code> global. Add
                    <code>// @@ts-check</code> to a module to have mistakes reported.
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.JavaScript" ViewerTitle="CounterWithProps.razor.js" class="mt-4">
// @@ts-check
/** @@type {import('rizzyui').AlpineCodeBehind&lt;{ initialCount: number, step: number }&gt;} */
export default () =&gt; ({
  count: 0,
  init() {
    this.count = this.$props.initialCount; // a number; this.$props.initalCount is an error
  },
});
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzParagraph class="mt-4">
                    To write modules in TypeScript, or to import npm packages from a module, turn on the build pipeline
                    described in <RzLink Href="/docs/interactivity/code-behind-deployment">Building and Deploying Code-Behind</RzLink>.
                </RzParagraph>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="Troubleshooting">
                    Troubleshooting
                </RzHeading>
                <ul class="list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 my-6 text-foreground">
                    <li><strong>Build warning <code>RZUI1001</code>:</strong> the component is marked <code>[RzAlpineCodeBehind]</code> but there is no <code>.razor.js</code> next to it. Check the file name: <code>Counter.razor.js</code> for <code>Counter.razor</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>An exception names <code>[RzAlpineCodeBehind]</code>:</strong> the component passed in <code>For</code> is not marked with the attribute.</li>
                    <li><strong>The console says <em>Could not load the code-behind module</em>:</strong> the URL in the message returned an error. Open it in the browser; the module must be next to the component so it is published as a static web asset. RizzyUI also dispatches <code>rz:module-error</code> on <code>document</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong><em>Two code-behind modules use the Alpine name</em>:</strong> two components set the same <code>Name</code>. Remove <code>Name</code> to use generated names.</li>
                    <li><strong>A property is "not defined":</strong> the markup uses a name the module does not return. Check the element's <code>x-data</code> and <code>x-load-src</code> in the browser's developer tools; the module is listed under its URL in the Sources panel.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <RzSeparator />

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="Next Steps">
                    Next Steps
                </RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    Continue with reactivity, <code>$watch</code>, <code>$refs</code> and stores in
                    <RzLink Href="/docs/interactivity/state-reactivity">Working with State and Reactivity</RzLink>, or read
                    <RzLink Href="/docs/interactivity/code-behind-deployment">Building and Deploying Code-Behind</RzLink>
                    for caching, deployments, TypeScript and testing.
                </RzParagraph>
            </section>

        </MainContent>
    </RzArticle>
</RzQuickReferenceContainer>
